  "text": "We are given the function:\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\frac{(x - 4)(x - 2)(x - 1)(x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 4)}{256} + 1.6\n$$\n\nWe are asked to **determine the maximum value of $ k $** such that the equation:\n\n$$\ng(x) = kx\n$$\n\nhas **exactly one solution**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the function\n\nThe function $ g(x) $ is a polynomial with leading term:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x - 4)(x - 2)(x - 1)(x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 4)}{256}\n$$\n\nThis is a degree 6 polynomial divided by 256. So the leading term is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^6}{256}\n$$\n\nSo, as $ x \\to \\infty $, $ g(x) \\to \\infty $, and as $ x \\to -\\infty $, $ g(x) \\to -\\infty $.\n\nWe are interested in the case where:\n\n$$\ng(x) = kx\n$$\n\nThis is a **linear equation** in $ x $, and we want to find the **maximum value of $ k $** such that the system:\n\n$$\ng(x) = kx \\quad \\text{and} \\quad g(x) = \\frac{(x - 4)(x - 2)(x - 1)(x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 4)}{256} + 1.6\n$$\n\nhas **exactly one solution**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Interpret the equation\n\nWe are solving:\n\n$$\ng(x) = kx\n\\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\frac{(x - 4)(x - 2)(x - 1)(x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 4)}{256} + 1.6 = kx\n$$\n\nThis is a **quadratic equation** in $ x $, because we are solving for $ x $ and $ k $, and the left-hand side is a polynomial.\n\nSo, for this equation to have **exactly one solution**, the **quadratic equation** in $ x $ must have **exactly one real root**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Set up the quadratic equation\n\nWe can rewrite the equation as:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x - 4)(x - 2)(x - 1)(x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 4)}{256} + 1.6 - kx = 0\n$$\n\nLet’s denote the left-hand side as a function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\frac{(x - 4)(x - 2)(x - 1)(x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 4)}{256} + 1.6 - kx\n$$\n\nWe want:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0 \\quad \\text{has exactly one solution.}\n$$\n\nThis means that the function $ f(x) $ touches the line $ y = kx $ at exactly one point.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: When does a quadratic have exactly one solution?\n\nA quadratic equation $ ax^2 + bx + c = 0 $ has **exactly one solution** when its discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\nb^2 - 4ac = 0\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, the equation is:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{(x - 4)(x - 2)(x - 1)(x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 4)}{256} + 1.6 - kx\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nLet’s consider the **leading term** of this equation. The leading term is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^6}{256}\n$$\n\nSo as $ x \\to \\infty $, the equation tends to infinity (for positive $ x $), and as $ x \\to -\\infty $, the equation tends to negative infinity.\n\nTherefore, the equation can only have **one solution** when the line $ y = kx $ **touches the graph of $ g(x) $** at exactly one point.\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Find the maximum $ k $\n\nTo find the **maximum** value of $ k $ such that the equation $ g(x) = kx $ has exactly one solution, we consider the **maximum value** of $ k $ such that the line $ y = kx $ **touches the graph**.\n\nThis occurs when the line is tangent to the graph at a point where $ g(x) = kx $.\n\nLet’s find $ k $ by solving:\n\n$$\ng(x) = kx \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\frac{(x - 4)(x - 2)(x - 1)(x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 4)}{256} + 1.6 = kx\n$$\n\nWe want this equation to have **exactly one solution**, so the line must be tangent to the graph at one point.\n\nTo find $ k $, we can consider the **maximum value** of $ k $ such that the function $ f(x) = g(x) - kx $ has **exactly one solution**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 6: Use calculus to find maximum $ k $\n\nLet’s define:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\frac{(x - 4)(x - 2)(x - 1)(x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 4)}{256} + 1.6 - kx\n$$\n\nWe want to find the maximum $ k $ such that $ f(x) = 0 $ has **exactly one solution**.\n\nWe can use the **maximum value** of $ k $ such that the line $ y = kx $ is tangent to the graph of $ g(x) $.\n\nThis happens when the system:\n\n$$\ng(x) = kx\n\\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\text{the graph of } g(x) \\text{ is tangent to } y = kx\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by finding the maximum value of $ k $ such that $ g(x) = kx $ has exactly one solution.\n\nThis happens when the line is tangent to the graph at a point, and the value of $ k $ is determined by the **slope of the graph** at that point.\n\n---\n\n### Step 7: Determine the maximum $ k $\n\nFrom the graph (as shown in the Asymptote code), we can see that the function $ g(x) $ is a polynomial with a **maximum value** of 1.6 at some point.\n\nSo the maximum value of $ k $ such that $ g(x) = kx $ has **exactly one solution** is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{1.6}\n$$\n\nThus, the **maximum value of $ k $** is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{1.6}\n$$",
